# ==============================================================================
# Line endings — D-100
#
# This repo is developed on Windows with `core.autocrlf=true`, which stores LF in the
# object database and checks out **CRLF into the working tree**. `sync.sh` packs from the
# working tree, so without this file every deploy from a Windows checkout ships CRLF text
# to a Linux host.
#
# That was not theoretical. `cuubz.service` reached `/var/www/html/` with CRLF while
# `/etc/systemd/system/cuubz.service` had LF, so step 7's
#
#     cmp -s ${REMOTE_DIR}/cuubz.service /etc/systemd/system/cuubz.service
#
# compared byte-identical text and found all 56 lines different. The `cmp` exists
# precisely so a normal deploy does NOT need a `daemon-reload`; with CRLF it reinstalls
# the unit and reloads systemd on **every single run**. It never surfaced on 10.0.30.160
# only because D-98 (no sudo) means step 7 never gets that far.
#
# The second-order risk is worse and worth naming: a CRLF unit file installed into /etc
# parses today only because systemd's `strstrip()` happens to drop the trailing `\r` from
# every value. That is a property of the parser, not a guarantee, and `ExecStart` carrying
# a stray `\r` is not a failure anyone wants to debug from a systemd error message.
#
# Scope is deliberate. `.sh` and `.service` are the files whose *bytes* have to be right
# on the far end — a shell interpreter and a systemd config parser both read them as
# text with meaningful line structure. JS/JSON/HTML are left alone: Node, browsers and
# Vite are all CRLF-tolerant, and `dist/` is generated with LF by the build anyway.
# ==============================================================================

*.sh        text eol=lf
*.service   text eol=lf
